//==
// # Primitives
//
// The types the Plato compiler assumes to exist BY NAME. Their representation comes
// from the runtime, not from these declarations, so none of them can be defined in
// Plato itself - which is exactly what the `primitive` keyword records (plato-367).
// Everything else in this vocabulary is an ordinary `type` whose shape is its
// declaration.
//
// Fields are intentionally absent throughout: a primitive has no declarable shape.
// What each declaration DOES carry is its concept surface - the `implements` list
// states which foundational concepts the intrinsic satisfies, and that is the only
// authority the checker has over these names.
//
// The set below is the whole set. Adding a declaration here is a claim that the
// compiler and every backend must know the name; see
// `Plato.Compiler/Types/WriterPrimitiveNames.cs` for the writer-side mirror and
// plato-365 for the standing effort to keep the set as small as possible.

// ## Scalars

// A 64-bit floating-point approximation of a real number.
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double-precision_floating-point_format)
primitive Number
    implements IReal
{ }

// A signed whole number. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integer_(computer_science))
primitive Integer
    implements IWhole, IBitwise
{ }

// A truth value: true or false. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_data_type)
primitive Boolean
    implements IValue, IOrderable, ILogical
{ }

// A single character of text. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Character_(computing))
primitive Character
    implements IValue, IOrderable
{ }

// Immutable text. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_(computer_science))
primitive String
    implements IValue, IOrderable, ICountable
{ }

// ## Type tokens

// A type reified as a value. Carries no members of its own; it exists so a static
// member can be reached through a type token (the `_: Type` receiver convention).
primitive Type
{ }

// ## Collections

// An immutable one-dimensional ordered collection.
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Array_(data_structure))
primitive Array<T>
    implements IIndexable<T>
{ }

// ### Affine builders
//
// The mutable half of the collection story. Every other collection in this
// vocabulary is immutable-by-value, which leaves no way to *build* one: nothing
// can accumulate points, sample a curve into an array, or assemble a mesh. The
// two builder types below fill that gap without giving up value semantics
// anywhere else, because uniqueness is confined to these two types.
// The `unique` modifier is an affine type discipline
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substructural_type_system).
//
// `unique` is a declaration-site modifier only. A *use* of `List<T>` or
// `Buffer<T>` is never annotated: the type itself carries the affine
// convention, so every pass of a builder value is a move. Only the intrinsic
// builder names `List` and `Buffer` may be declared unique; the compiler
// hard-rejects the modifier on any other type.
//
// Method effects (the compiler's `UniqueTypes` intrinsic table):
//
// | Effect  | Members            | Meaning                                          |
// |---------|--------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
// | observe | `Count`, `At`      | free use; does not invalidate the builder        |
// | mutate  | `Add`, `AddRange`, `Set` | returns the builder - rebind or chain      |
// | consume | `Freeze`           | zero-copy handoff to an immutable `Array<T>`; the builder is invalidated |
//
// Conventions, runtime-checked today (the static affine pass rides the future
// type-checker workstream - see `../docs/affine-types.md`):
//
// - never store a builder in a field (LINT006) or inside another generic
//   container (LINT007);
// - never capture a builder in a lambda;
// - write `Freeze` with parentheses: `xs.Freeze()`;
// - construct with `new List<T>()` / `new Buffer<T>(n)` at concrete element
//   types, or `xs.EmptyList()` where the element type is a type variable;
// - rebind or chain every mutator (`xs = xs.Add(p)`, or
//   `new List<Integer>().Add(1).Add(2).Freeze()`). Use after `Freeze()` throws
//   at runtime.
//
// The backing runtimes are handwritten (`Plato.Intrinsics/PlatoList.cs`,
// `PlatoBuffer.cs`); the compiler maps `List`/`Buffer` to `PlatoList`/
// `PlatoBuffer` and never generates a struct for a unique type.
//
// The bodiless host signatures for these two types live in
// `intrinsics.library.plato`.

// A growable affine builder: append elements, then promote the backing store to
// an immutable Array<T> with Freeze(). (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_array)
unique primitive List<T>
{ }

// A fixed-size affine builder: allocate n slots, write them by index in any
// order, then promote the backing store to an immutable Array<T> with Freeze().
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_buffer)
unique primitive Buffer<T>
{ }

// ## Function types
//
// Pure function values, one per arity 0-9. TR is the result type; T0..T8 are the
// parameter types in order. The compiler resolves a lambda of N parameters to
// FunctionN, so the arity ceiling here is the language's ceiling.

primitive Function0<TR> { }
primitive Function1<T0, TR> { }
primitive Function2<T0, T1, TR> { }
primitive Function3<T0, T1, T2, TR> { }
primitive Function4<T0, T1, T2, T3, TR> { }
primitive Function5<T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, TR> { }
primitive Function6<T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, TR> { }
primitive Function7<T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, TR> { }
primitive Function8<T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7, TR> { }
primitive Function9<T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, TR> { }
